[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-39", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 39 — JFR names two families; the drive chooses grouped or global by expected steps; 64 slots 4374 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-39.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 39 - the profile names two families; the drive learns to choose its walk; a count-only rule refuted the same hour\n\n> Seed: iteration 38's S30. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung :sf1-full;\n> SUT levi (10 cores, 16 threads, G1 IHOP 75), driver judah, LAN.\n\n## Generate / Rank\n\n  S30: JFR, 40 s, the SUT under the 64-slot mix (4,217 samples). Two\n  families: the binding-set machinery of the generic join (transient hash\n  maps 13%, hasheq of binding maps and vectors 11%, array-map lookups 7%)\n  and the grouped drive's cuts (ve-compare 8% + PersistentTreeMap.seqFrom\n  6%) -- one cut per group, and IC09 reaches ~2,200 groups for 20 rows.\n\n  Hypothesis: for a large group set the GLOBAL value walk is cheaper --\n  it fills the limit in about limit * N_e / E_G steps (entries of the\n  ordering attribute over entries in the reached groups): IC09's groups\n  hold 22% of all messages, so ~90 steps against 2,200 cuts.\n\n  First rule, on group counts alone (|G|^2 < limit * N): IC09 in process\n  8.7 -> 4.9 ms, 0 disagreements -- and the served sweep at 64 slots fell\n  to 35% on-time with IC07 at 81 ms p50 / 510 p95. IC07's 2,000 message\n  groups hold 0.2% of all likes: the global walk exhausted its 512-value\n  budget and fell to the plain join. Refuted the same hour; receipt kept.\n  Second rule, on the entry ratio (global only when the expected steps\n  are under both half the budget and |G|): IC07 back at its grouped\n  cost, IC09 keeps the global walk.\n\n    rung sf1-full  slots  threads  reads/s  upd/s  on-time%  thru/s\n      it.38          64     16      2944     1475   100.00    4233.3\n      it.39          64     16      2944     1475   100.00    4374.1   (3328: 73.4% fails)\n\n  IC09 wire p50 at 64 slots 30.7 -> 20.0 ms; the column +3% at the same\n  passing read rate. Tests: datalog JVM 136/422/0, cljs 140/433/0;\n  kotobase-peer 262/803/0.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - A cost model with the wrong denominator is worse than no model: the\n    count rule was right on the op it was built from and wrong on the\n    next op over. The refutation cost one sweep because the sweep is\n    always run before the row is written; the rule that survived has\n    the quantity that actually sets the hit rate.\n  - IC09 served alone at 8 slots is still ~12 ms p50 where the same code\n    in one process is 4.9 on the same parameters -- unexplained, and\n    now a named seed (S31) rather than a number to argue about.\n  - The other profile family, the generic join's binding sets, is the\n    engine's next constant-factor lever (S32): hasheq of binding maps is\n    paid on every `into #{}`.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH, small (64-slot column\n4233 -> 4374 at the same passing rate). Ratio to the audited No.1: 32.\n\nSeeds for iteration 40, in order:\n\n  - S31: why served IC09 is 2.5x its in-process time.\n  - S32: the binding-set machinery (hasheq of binding maps; vectors as\n    bindings inside the drive).\n  - M4: more cores. S26: full-data parameters. S23b: IC05. M3: SF3.\n"}]
